## Creating authorized_keys

Create the file:

```bash
touch ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

Set proper permissions:

```bash
chmod 600 ~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

Verify:

```bash
ls -la ~/.ssh
```

Expected output:

```text
-rw------- 1 finesse finesse 0 Jun 9 08:50 authorized_keys
```

---

## Adding a Public Key

Add a public key:

```text
ssh-ed25519 AAAAC3NzaC1lZDI1NTE5AAAA.... user@laptop
```

into:

```text
~/.ssh/authorized_keys
```

Each key must be on its own line.

Example:

```text
ssh-ed25519 AAAA... laptop
ssh-rsa AAAA... desktop
ssh-ed25519 AAAA... mobaxterm
```

---

## Multiple Users or Devices

You can authorize multiple devices by adding multiple public keys.

Example:

```text
ssh-ed25519 AAAA... laptop
ssh-ed25519 BBBB... office-pc
ssh-rsa CCCC... mobaxterm
```

Anyone possessing the matching private key can log in.

